{
  "id": "indefinitpronomen",
  "name": "some, any, no and somebody, anything, nowhere",
  "level": "A2",
  "status": "entwurf",
  "subtitle": "Use some in positive sentences and offers, any in negatives and questions, and no instead of not any: I've got some tea. I haven't got any milk. There is no sugar.",
  "briefMarks": [
    "some",
    "any",
    "no",
    "not any"
  ],
  "source": "EnglishAcademy original English text (grammar-a2 run, 2026-10-29)",
  "concept": {
    "flow": [
      {
        "text": "positive, offer · some",
        "variant": "verb"
      },
      {
        "text": "negative, question · any",
        "variant": "prep"
      },
      {
        "text": "no = not any"
      }
    ],
    "exampleHtml": "There is <span class=\"vp-verb\">some</span> tea. — There isn't <span class=\"vp-prep\">any</span> milk. — Is there <span class=\"vp-prep\">any</span> sugar? — No, there is no sugar.",
    "qa": [
      {
        "html": "<strong>some</strong> is for positive sentences. Use it with plural nouns and uncountable nouns: <em>I've got some apples. There is some milk. I can see something in the box.</em>",
        "tag": "some"
      },
      {
        "html": "<strong>any</strong> is for negatives and questions: <em>I haven't got any money. Have you got any brothers? Is there anything to eat?</em>",
        "tag": "any"
      },
      {
        "html": "In <strong>offers</strong> and <strong>requests</strong> we expect the answer yes, so we use <strong>some</strong>: <em>Would you like some tea? Can I have some water, please? Would you like something to drink?</em>",
        "tag": "Offers and requests"
      },
      {
        "html": "<strong>no</strong> means <em>not any</em>. The verb stays positive: <em>There is no milk.</em> (= <em>There isn't any milk.</em>) It is the same with <strong>nobody, nothing, nowhere</strong>: <em>Nobody is here. I've got nothing to wear.</em> Use only one negative in a sentence.",
        "tag": "no, nobody, nothing, nowhere"
      },
      {
        "html": "Add <strong>-body</strong> or <strong>-one</strong> for people, <strong>-thing</strong> for things and <strong>-where</strong> for places. <em>somebody</em> and <em>someone</em> mean the same, and so do <em>anybody / anyone</em>, <em>everybody / everyone</em> and <em>nobody / no one</em>. The verb is singular: <em>Everybody is here. Nothing is wrong.</em>",
        "tag": "-body, -one, -thing, -where"
      },
      {
        "html": "✗ <em>I don't have some money.</em> → ✓ <em>I don't have any money.</em> · ✗ <em>I don't know nothing.</em> → ✓ <em>I don't know anything.</em> · ✗ <em>Everybody are here.</em> → ✓ <em>Everybody is here.</em>",
        "tag": "Typical mistakes"
      }
    ],
    "noteHtml": "<em>Any</em> can also be in a positive sentence when the choice does not matter: <em>Take any book you like.</em> At A2, learn the simple rule first: <em>some</em> in positive sentences and offers, <em>any</em> in negatives and questions. More about negatives is in <a href=\"/negation\">Negatives</a>. For <em>much, many, a few</em> and <em>a little</em>, see <a href=\"/satzstellung\">Countable and uncountable nouns</a>.",
